      "restoredAt": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I think this may also be related to cultural differences and social norms. In Japanese and Chinese cultures, people tend to avoid touching others casually and often check whether someone is comfortable with physical contact first.\n\nIf someone’s nameplate shows the Avatar Interaction Disabled icon, they may simply avoid touching them without necessarily asking for permission, maintaining a comfortable social distance.\n\nBecause of this, I think this icon may be particularly important for Japanese and Chinese users.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
